    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe power of product rule states that if we want to raise the product [MATH: xy] to some power [MATH: n], we must raise each factor to the power [MATH: n] and then multiply:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (xy)^n=x^n y^n]\n\nContent:\nFor example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (xy)^4=x^4y^4.]\n\nContent:\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (xy)^{4}|=; (xy)(xy)(xy)(xy)|=; (x \u22c5 x \u22c5 x \u22c5 x)(y \u22c5 y \u22c5 y \u22c5 y)|=; x^{4}y^{4}]\n\nContent:\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Using the Power of Product Rule with a Positive Power\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\left(2x\\right)^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n[MATH: (2x)^{3}|=; (2)^{3} \u22c5 (x)^{3}|=; (8) \u22c5 (x^{3})|=; 8x^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (\u2212 2a)^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6a^{3}]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 8a^{3}]\nc. [MATH: 8a^{3}]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 6a^{3}]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 2a^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (3x)^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3x]\nb. [MATH: 6x]\nc. [MATH: 3x^{2}]\nd. [MATH: 6x^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 9x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Using the Power of Product Rule with a Negative Power\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\left(pq^{-2}\\right)^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n[MATH: (pq^{\u2212 2})^{3}|=; p^{3} \u22c5 (q^{\u2212 2})^{3}|=; p^{3} \u22c5 q^{(\u2212 2)(3)}|=; p^{3}q^{\u2212 6}|=; (p^{3})/(q^{6})]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (x^{\u2212 2}y^{6})^{\u2212 2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (y^{4})/(x^{4})]\nb. [MATH: xy^{4}]\nc. [MATH: xy]\nd. [MATH: x^{4}y^{8}]\ne. [MATH: (x^{4})/(y^{12})]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (a^{\u2212 1}b)^{\u2212 7} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (a^{7})/(b^{7})]\nb. [MATH: (ab)^{8}]\nc. [MATH: a^{7}b^{7}]\nd. [MATH: a^{8}b^{\u2212 8}]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(a^{7}b^{7})]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Using the Power of Product Rule with Three Factors\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (2x^2y^{-1})^{-4}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n[MATH: (2x^{2}y^{\u2212 1})^{\u2212 4}|=; 2^{\u2212 4} \u22c5 (x^{2})^{\u2212 4} \u22c5 (y^{\u2212 1})^{\u2212 4}|=; (1)/(2^{4}) \u22c5 x^{(2)(\u2212 4)} \u22c5 y^{(\u2212 1)(\u2212 4)}|=; (1)/(16) \u22c5 x^{\u2212 8} \u22c5 y^{4}|=; (1)/(16) \u22c5 (1)/(x^{8}) \u22c5 y^{4}|=; (y^{4})/(16x^{8})]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: (10u^{\u2212 2}v^{\u2212 3})^{\u2212 3}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (u^{5}v^{6})/(1000)]\nb. [MATH: (u^{6}v^{9})/(30)]\nc. [MATH: (1000)/(u^{6}v^{9})]\nd. [MATH: (30)/(u^{6}v^{9})]\ne. [MATH: (u^{6}v^{9})/(1000)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (a^{2}b^{\u2212 1}c^{2})^{\u2212 2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (b^{2}c^{4})/(a^{4})]\nb. [MATH: (b^{2})/(a^{4}c^{4})]\nc. [MATH: (a^{4}b^{2})/(c^{4})]\nd. [MATH: (a^{4}c^{4})/(b^{2})]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(b^{3})]"
